thank you very much good morning  after the distressing images we've seen  i'd like to address the question what  expert institutions like the engineers  museum  in berlin can actually do what measures  they can take  to  address the issue of protecting the  cultural heritage of iraq and syria at  home and more specifically what we are  doing at the moment  i would like to touch briefly upon four  points actually integrated object care  and presentation  fighting the illicit traffic in cultural  property on a national level bilateral  capacity building programs and finally  finally raising awareness for cultural  property per dec protection  let me start by briefly introducing the  engineering museum located in the  pergamon museum on berlin's museum  island  the engineers museum houses a collection  of about 500 000 objects from ancient  mesopotamian  society so it's a collection of  reference for these ancient mesopotamian  societies we have signature collections  from iraq and syria namely asur babylon  uruk  telhalaf kabira to name only a few  and it's important to point out that 95  percent of these collections come from  regular archaeological excavations  mostly at the beginning of the 20th  century  and they were transferred to berlin on  the basis of bilateral division  agreements with the respective source  countries  we have been a leading research  institution with a focus on  object-oriented research and in our  mission statement we say that we  consider ourselves a public repository  for the archaeological heritage of iraq  and syria  of course dealing with this heritage and  protecting it starts in our storage  facilities so what we're doing of course  is something that i would like to call  the integrated object care and  presentation when i say integrated i  mean that these objects are not only  considered by us as archaeological  objects or pieces of art but also as  cultural heritage of iraq and syria and  this integrated object care and  presentation includes state-of-the-art  storage  preventive conservation digital  documentation especially in the area of  3d digitization basic research very  important provenance research on the  history of the individual collections  and a multi-perspective  presentation  the second area of activity i would like  to comment upon is fighting the illicit  traffic we've just inaugurated a large  national research project focusing on  the illicit traffic with archaeological  objects in germany the project is called  illicid and in london i should probably  point out that this is not a mistake not  a spelling mistake but an acronym for a  german title  we're undertaking an explorative study  with special attention to archaeological  objects from iraq and syria which means  we're  looking into object types  turnovers networks operation modes etc  very important is that this is a  transdisciplinary research design which  also relies on non-academic partners  especially law enforcement and customs  authorities  our associated partners include the  federal foreign office the federal  commissioner for culture and media the  german commission for unesco and of  course i come with it international  observatory of illicit trafficking the  project is financed by the federal  ministry for education and research  generously with 1.2  million  third  area of activity that's important for us  is what we call bilateral capacity  building programs together with our  partners from iraq we're about to  initiate  an iraqi german expert dialogue on  iraq's cultural heritage at  archaeological sites and museums icasm  is supposed to be a bilateral expert  panel for the planning and coordination  of a large-scale capacity building  cooperation program between iraq and  germany so we're talking about an expert  panel that is setting out methodological  and operational guidelines defining  infrastructural political legal  scientific and operational requirements  for a sustainable exchange and  development of knowledge in iraq that is  important the project was initiated by  the permanent delegate of iraq to unesco  and the ancient near east museum the  partners here include the german  commission for unesco and the orient  department of the german archaeological  institute as well as i can financial  support comes from the federal foreign  office  and my final and last point as a public  institution we know that we have to  raise awareness for the cultural  property protection agenda as a whole  and we're doing that on a lot of  different fields and one example of  course  is  our public support for the ongoing  revision of the german cultural property  protection law from 2007 most of you  will know that the new law is aiming at  better import controls more effective  return mechanisms and strict due  diligence principles related to the eu  directive 60 2014  the new law also envisions export  documentation by the respective source  countries for all archaeological objects  to be imported to germany this is very  important if it actually happens it will  change things completely and the german  government has  pleaded or actually  recommended that the  eu council also deal with this problem  and implements corresponding measures on  the eu level because it is clear that as  long as there is illicit trafficking  with archaeological objects from iraq  and syria it will not be able to protect  this cultural heritage effectively in  the future thank you very much
 

